From 3f6f7ee022644bf913987f37ca683edbdb6c464e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joseph Stackhouse <joseph.stackhouse@stack-tech.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 06:01:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] seed prompt graph executables with a capture-only first
 observation

The prompt path submitted every compatible graph directly from a single host
thread, and prior attribution placed the large majority of sampled server CPU
cycles inside driver submission rather than arithmetic. On a narrow link with
mostly idle cards, that submission cost dominates.

On the first observation of a prompt graph identity this captures and
instantiates a graph executable but deliberately does not launch it; that same
observation still executes through the normal direct path. Only later
observations replay. Prompt graphs use a namespace separate from decode graphs
and the prompt entries are released at the first batch-one decode graph.

That ordering is the safety property: a mutable first observation is never
replayed from an unproven executable. Executables are keyed by first node and
prompt namespace rather than by source address, which fragmented at long
context.

The measured effect is strongly configuration-dependent. One tested model
family gained a large multiple of its prompt throughput while another gained a
few percent, and one showed a small batch-one decode cost. It is not
represented as a decode gain.

Default-off, selected by GGML_CUDA_PROMPT_GRAPH_CAPTURE_SEED=1. Deterministic
output is retained.
